class:: BlipAD
summary:: Periodic finite-bandwidth attack-decay excitation
categories:: UGens>Generators>Deterministic
related:: Guides/BlipExtensions, Classes/BlipDecay, Classes/BlipDiff, Classes/BlipDisp, Classes/BlipFB, Classes/BlipFrac, Classes/BlipInt, Classes/BlipKernel, Classes/BlipPM, Classes/BlipPeak, Classes/BlipPower, Classes/BlipSeries, Classes/BlipWidth

description::

BlipAD sends a peak-normalized finite Dirichlet impulse train, including DC,
into two one-pole responses and subtracts them. The resulting difference has
a fast attack lobe, a slower tail, and a smaller opposite-polarity recovery
lobe because both one-poles have unity DC gain.

Attack and decay are sampled once per block. Each is at least one sample and
decay is forced to remain at least one sample longer than attack. Coefficients
and a normalization derived from the discrete difference-of-exponentials
response interpolate over the block. That gain has a unity safety ceiling
because a low-bandwidth Dirichlet pulse can span many samples rather than
behaving like one isolated sample. Repeated pulses overlap naturally. States
are denormal-cleared and never dynamically normalized from the output.
The finite excitation harmonics are limited below Nyquist; the two linear
one-poles change their magnitudes but do not generate new sidebands.

The closest vanilla building blocks are link::Classes/Blip:: and
link::Classes/OnePole::. BlipAD combines their finite excitation and smoothing
topologies with internal limiting and deterministic state.

classmethods::

method:: ar

argument:: freq
Pulse repetition frequency in Hz. Audio or control rate; signed values are
valid.

argument:: numharm
Excitation harmonic count, sampled once per block and clipped to
code::1..2048:: before limiting.

argument:: attack
Fast one-pole time constant in seconds, sampled once per block.

argument:: decay
Slow one-pole time constant in seconds, sampled once per block and kept above
code::attack::.

argument:: mul
Post-processing multiplier; it does not enter filter state.

argument:: add
Post-processing offset; it does not enter filter state.

examples::

code::
(
{ BlipAD.ar(8, 80, 0.002, 0.08, 0.2) }.play;
)
::

code::
(
{ Ringz.ar(BlipAD.ar(5, 100, 0.001, 0.03), 1400, 0.2) * 0.1 }.play;
)
::

code::
// Modulating all parameters for a dynamic sound
(
{ BlipAD.ar(LFNoise1.kr(0.5).exprange(50, 400), MouseY.kr(1, 100), LFNoise1.kr(1).range(0.001, 0.1), MouseX.kr(0.01, 1.0)) ! 2 * 0.1 }.play;
)
::
